The trace norm of a non-Hermitian operator $A$ is defined as the sum of its singular values $\sum \sigma_i$. For a Hermitian operator, this is equivalent to the sum of the absolute values of its eigenvalues $\sum |\lambda_i|$. 

The original implementation of `Qobj.norm(norm='tr')` was incorrectly using the eigenvalues of the operator $A$ for both Hermitian and non-Hermitian operators, which is only correct for the Hermitian case. For non-Hermitian operators, it was calculating $\sum |\lambda_i|$, which is not the trace norm.

I have updated the `norm` method in `qutip/qobj.py` to correctly handle non-Hermitian operators when `norm='tr'`. For non-Hermitian operators, it now calculates the singular values by finding the eigenvalues of $A^\dagger A$ (which are $\sigma_i^2$) and then takes the sum of their square roots ($\sum \sqrt{\sigma_i^2} = \sum \sigma_i$). This approach utilizes the existing sparse eigenvalue solver (`sp_eigs`) on the Hermitian operator $A^\dagger A$, which is efficient and handles sparse matrices correctly.

