These changes allow `CSVLogger` to support nested results by creating headers and rows with flattened keys (e.g., `train/loss`).

The issue was that `CSVLogger` did not support nested dictionaries in the `result` object, writing them as strings instead of flattening them into delimited keys (e.g., `train/loss`).

I have implemented a `_flatten_dict` helper function that recursively flattens a dictionary using `/` as a separator. I then updated `CSVLogger.on_result` to use this function to flatten the `result` dictionary before writing it to the CSV file. This ensures that nested results are correctly represented as individual columns in the output CSV, consistent with how `TFLogger` handles them.

