The issue was caused by a "soft" import of `sklearn.utils.shuffle`. When `scikit-learn` was not installed, the code caught the `ModuleNotFoundError` (or `ImportError`), issued a warning, and continued execution. This led to a `NameError` later in the `UnlabeledImagenet` class when `shuffle` was called, as the name had never been defined.

I have modified the import block in `pl_bolts/datasets/imagenet_dataset.py` to raise an `ImportError` instead of just issuing a warning. This ensures that the program fails immediately at import time with a clear error message if the dependency is missing, consistent with the user's expectation and preventing the subsequent `NameError`. I also changed `ModuleNotFoundError` to `ImportError` to be more robust across different Python versions and import scenarios.

