I have replaced the `@contextlib.contextmanager` implementation of `Scheduler._task_context` with a dedicated `_TaskContext` class. This change avoids the overhead of the `contextlib` decorator and the associated generator creation on every call, which was identified as a performance bottleneck when scheduling tasks.

The new `_TaskContext` class implements the context manager protocol directly, minimizing the number of function calls and object allocations required during task scheduling.

```python
    def _task_context(self, task):
